<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helmut Kohl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helmut Kohl was a German Christian Democratic politician who was Chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> and then of reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1982 to 1998, the longest-serving chancellor since Bismarck. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a> he pushed rapidly for reunification, achieved on 3 October 1990. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> he drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-integration">European integration</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a> and the euro.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl.jpg" alt="Helmut Kohl" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Lambiotte · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmut_Kohl_(1996)_cropped_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helmut Kohl was born on 3 April 1930 in Ludwigshafen, the third child of Hans Kohl, a Bavarian army veteran and civil servant, and Cäcilie Kohl. He joined the Christian Democratic Union in 1946, at the age of 16. In 1958 he took a doctorate in history at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">Heidelberg University</a>, and then worked for a foundry and for the Industrial Union for Chemistry.</p><p>He was elected the youngest member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a> parliament in 1959. On 19 May 1969 he became minister-president of that state. As a reformer he focused on schools and education: his government abolished school corporal punishment and the parochial school, and he founded the University of Trier-Kaiserslautern. In 1973 he was elected national chairman of the CDU.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kohl led the opposition under Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-schmidt">Helmut Schmidt</a>. The Free Democratic Party broke with its coalition partner in 1982, and on 1 October 1982 a constructive vote of no confidence deposed Schmidt, the only time a chancellor has been removed in that way. Three days later the Bundestag elected Kohl chancellor of a CDU/CSU-FDP coalition. His government stationed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> mid-range missiles despite strong opposition from the peace movement.</p><p>On 22 September 1984 Kohl met the French president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, and their long handshake became a symbol of Franco-German reconciliation. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a> his government acted decisively, and reunification followed in 1990. With Mitterrand he was an architect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a>, which established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and the euro. He moved the federal capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, though government offices relocated only in 1999. He lost the 1998 election to Gerhard Schröder and resigned as party leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1999 it emerged that the CDU had received and kept illegal donations during Kohl's leadership; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/der-spiegel">Der Spiegel</a> reported that he had not benefited personally but had run the party's finances outside the legal boundaries. His reputation suffered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, though abroad he was still regarded as a European statesman. In 1998 the European heads of state or government named him Honorary Citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, an honour previously given only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-monnet">Jean Monnet</a>. Kohl died on 16 June 2017 in Ludwigshafen, aged 87, and was buried in Speyer after a European act of state in Strasbourg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helmut Kohl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Germany’s Basic Law lets the Bundestag remove a chancellor only by electing a successor in the same vote, a rule meant to avoid the crises of the Weimar Republic; it has succeeded once, when <b>Helmut Kohl</b> replaced Helmut Schmidt in 1982.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The East German premier Lothar de Maizière proposed adding Becher's words to the united anthem; the West German chancellor <b>Helmut Kohl</b> rejected the idea.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote>…1990 the Federal Constitutional Court ruled that only the third stanza is protected by criminal law as the anthem, and in November 1991 President Richard von Weizsäcker and Chancellor <b>Helmut Kohl</b> agreed in an exchange of letters that the third stanza alone is the anthem of reunified Germany.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Friesenheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Ludwigshafen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.5,8.42&amp;year=1986" aria-label="Friesenheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4744 439 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Helmut Kohl"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.5,8.42&amp;year=1986">What was here in 1986? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
